{"schema_version":"blind-4.2.0","winner":"A","margin":"small_but_meaningful","reason":"A's turns actually respond to each other: the cost of discarding is answered by the cost of prolonged stinging, a concrete pause-and-reintroduce schedule follows, and the Minimalist concedes for a named reason and narrows to a patch test, so the ending knows more than the opening. B mostly repeats positions (Popper isolates, Thoreau simplifies, Clear stops everything) and converges on the same plan without any objection reshaping it. A's lead is tempered by its Pragmatist floating 'stinging as progress' without conditions, which a user could misread as permission to push through.","comparisons":[{"aspect":"Reasoning and interaction","a_location":"A.debate[4].text, A.debate[5].text, A.debate[6].text, A.debate[8].text","a_observation":"The Skeptic objects to discarding on cost grounds; the Minimalist answers with the cost of prolonged irritation; the Pragmatist proposes a 4-day pause then one product every 3 days; the Minimalist drops her claim, citing the Skeptic's argument about future waste, and narrows to a patch test on the inner arm.","b_location":"B.debate[4].text, B.debate[7].text, B.debate[9].text, B.debate[10].text","b_observation":"The Skeptic restates isolation three times; the Minimalist answers with aphorisms rather than the objection; the Efficiency Expert repeats his turn-3 plan in turn 9 and the Skeptic simply agrees. No claim narrows because of an objection.","advantage":"A","why_it_matters":"The user asked how to figure out the cause; A arrives at a staged, observable procedure through the exchange, while B's plan is stated at turn 3 and never tested or refined."},{"aspect":"Usefulness for this decision","a_location":"A.dependsOn","a_observation":"Branches on visible inflammation or broken skin (stop and consult a professional), stinging without damage (systematic reintroduction), and single-product vs combination uncertainty (patch test).","b_location":"B.dependsOn","b_observation":"Branches only on severe vs mild irritation; the mild branch gives stop-to-baseline then one-by-one reintroduction with 'several days' between.","advantage":"A","why_it_matters":"A gives the user observable conditions (visible damage) and an escalation path to a professional, plus a lower-exposure test; B's branches are coarser and omit when to seek help."},{"aspect":"Trust and naturalness","a_location":"A.debate[3].text, A.debate[9].text, A.clearer","a_observation":"The Pragmatist suggests stinging may be a temporary adjustment, backed by a James quote about truth that does not bear on skin; this unconditioned possibility is carried into the closing as an open disagreement. Several turns open with the same 'You might...' template.","b_location":"B.debate[2].text, B.debate[5].text, B.debate[8].text","b_observation":"Thoreau's lines are on-theme and the three voices differ in register, but the quotes decorate rather than argue, and B.debate[4].text ('keep the others constant') is left ambiguous about whether to keep using two products.","advantage":"tie","why_it_matters":"Each has a calibration weakness; A's is more consequential for safety, B's for clarity, and neither decides the comparison over A's stronger reasoning."}],"best_of_a":{"location":"A.debate[8].text","observation":"An earned concession that names its cause and narrows the proposal to a patch test before reintroduction, a concrete lower-risk step the user can take."},"best_of_b":{"location":"B.debate[7].text","observation":"States the falsification point crisply: stopping everything never shows which single product is safe, which is the real reason to reintroduce one at a time."},"concerns":[{"side":"A","type":"safety","severity":"material","status":"observed","location":"A.debate[3].text","description":"Suggests stinging may be a sign of progress and the products may be working, without naming when that could apply (e.g., known actives) or when it should not be tolerated; A.clearer keeps it as an unresolved possibility, which could encourage continued use of an irritant.","evidence_ids":[]},{"side":"A","type":"grounding","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"A.debate[3].text","description":"William James's quotation about truth as the expedient is applied to skin reactions, a stretch that lends unearned authority to the 'adjustment' claim.","evidence_ids":[]},{"side":"B","type":"format","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"B.debate[2].text, B.debate[5].text, B.debate[8].text","description":"Thoreau quotations are given in first person ('As I wrote') without quotation marks, blurring the book's words with the mind's own speech.","evidence_ids":[]},{"side":"both","type":"uncertainty","severity":"minor","status":"observed","location":"A.dependsOn, B.dependsOn","description":"Neither asks which products or active ingredients were introduced, a fact the user did not give that would materially change which product to suspect first.","evidence_ids":[]}]}
